# Append-only records get a UNION merge.
#
# These files are written by concurrent workflows — the recorder runs a
# long-lived job while the agent, discovery and dev-leg all commit to data/ —
# and every writer only ever appends. When two jobs append different rows, a
# normal rebase stops with a conflict and leaves the repository mid-rebase.
# Every subsequent git command in that job then fails, and because the
# recorder's flush deliberately survives push failures, it kept recording and
# silently stopped committing for the rest of its run. Two multi-hour gaps in
# the price series came from exactly that.
#
# A union merge keeps both sides' lines, which is the correct resolution for an
# append-only log and turns the conflict into a non-event.
#
# ONLY append-only line-based files belong here. A union merge on JSON would
# produce a file with both versions concatenated and no valid syntax, so
# latest.json, constituents.json and the rest are deliberately absent.
data/prices.csv merge=union
data/agent/distance.csv merge=union
